/-! # Finite type field points

Source: Stacks Project, Morphisms, Tag 01TA (lemma-point-finite-type), conditions
(1), (2), (4), and the canonical residue-field point consequence
(blueprint: lem-sfb-finite-type-field-point-characterization).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A field-valued point is of finite type if and only if it is locally of finite type,
and if and only if its image is closed in some affine open and its induced residue-field
extension is finite. Under these conditions the canonical residue-field point is of finite type.
Closedness is taken in the affine open, with its subspace topology. The field embedding is
`V.descResidueField (Scheme.stalkClosedPointTo f)`, not an independently chosen algebra structure.

Source: Stacks Project, Morphisms, Tag 01TA (lemma-point-finite-type), conditions
(1), (2), (4), and its closed-point factorization
(blueprint: lem-sfb-finite-type-field-point-characterization).

Proof:
1. Spec l has exactly one point. The preimage of every affine open is empty or this
   point, hence quasi-compact. Thus finite type and locally finite type are equivalent
   (def-weil-affine-spectrum, def-weil-finite-type-morphism). The same applies to Spec κ(v).
2. If f is locally of finite type, choose an affine open Spec A containing its image v.
   By lem-weil-pbc-lft-affine-pairs, A → l is of finite type. By
   lem-weil-pbc-spec-field-point-residue-field its kernel is the prime p corresponding
   to v and κ(v) is the fraction field of A/p, embedded in l. The same generators
   generate l over A/p. Apply lem-sfb-field-finite-type-over-subring-localization to
   obtain a nonzero s in A/p such that (A/p)[1/s] is a field over which l is finite.
3. Lift s to a in A. By lem-weil-pbc-affine-open-section-vanishing-prime, v lies in the
   affine open D(a), whose ring is A[1/a]. By lem-weil-pbc-localization-quotient-commute,
   the quotient by the extended prime is (A/p)[1/s]. This is a field, so the prime is
   maximal and v is closed in D(a). This field equals the fraction field of A/p,
   since it is a field localization inside that fraction field. Thus it is κ(v),
   compatibly with its embedding in l, and l/κ(v) is finite.
4. Conversely, let v be closed in an affine open Spec A. Its prime p is maximal:
   a maximal ideal containing p would otherwise give a different point in its closure.
   By lem-weil-pbc-spec-field-point-residue-field, κ(v) = A/p. A finite κ(v)-basis of l
   generates l as an A-algebra, since coefficients lift along A → A/p. The sole point
   of Spec l therefore satisfies lem-weil-pbc-lft-pointwise-criterion, so f is locally
   of finite type.
5. In the same affine open the canonical Spec κ(v) → V is induced by A → A/p.
   This quotient is generated by the empty family as an A-algebra. By
   lem-weil-pbc-lft-pointwise-criterion it is locally of finite type, and by step 1
   it is quasi-compact, hence finite type (def-weil-finite-type-morphism). -/
theorem finite_type_field_point_characterization
    (V : Scheme.{u}) (l : Type u) [Field l] (f : Spec (.of l) ⟶ V) :
    ((LocallyOfFiniteType f ∧ QuasiCompact f) ↔ LocallyOfFiniteType f) ∧
    (LocallyOfFiniteType f ↔
      ∃ W : V.Opens, IsAffineOpen W ∧ f (IsLocalRing.closedPoint l) ∈ W ∧
        IsClosed (Subtype.val ⁻¹' {f (IsLocalRing.closedPoint l)} : Set W) ∧
        RingHom.Finite (V.descResidueField (Scheme.stalkClosedPointTo f)).hom) ∧
    (LocallyOfFiniteType f →
      LocallyOfFiniteType (V.fromSpecResidueField (f (IsLocalRing.closedPoint l))) ∧
      QuasiCompact (V.fromSpecResidueField (f (IsLocalRing.closedPoint l)))) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
